  introBody={[
    "When a child eats five foods, gags at new textures, or turns every dinner into a battle, parents are often told to \"just keep offering.\" We dig deeper. Feeding is one of the most complex things the body does — it involves sensory processing, oral motor strength, airway function, posture, and emotional safety, all at once.",
    "Our feeding team evaluates the whole system. Is an unaddressed tongue tie or low oral tone making chewing exhausting? Is mouth breathing killing appetite and taste? Is sensory over-responsivity making textures genuinely unbearable? The answer shapes the treatment — and it's why our approach works when 'exposure alone' hasn't.",
    "Whether your child is a picky eater, a problem feeder, or navigating swallowing (dysphagia) concerns, therapy is gentle, play-based, and pressure-free. Parents are coached at every step, because mealtime happens at your table, not ours.",
  ]}
  sideImage="/images/stock-baby-eating.png"
  sideImageAlt="Baby exploring food during a feeding therapy program"
  signsHeading="Signs your child may benefit from feeding therapy"
  signs={[
    'Eats fewer than ~15-20 foods, or drops foods without adding new ones',
    'Gags, coughs, or vomits with certain textures',
    'Mealtimes regularly end in tears — theirs or yours',
    'Chews with an open mouth, pockets food, or swallows pieces whole',
    'Refuses entire food groups or temperatures',
    'Long mealtimes (30-45+ minutes) or grazing all day',
    'Poor weight gain or concerns from your pediatrician',
    'History of reflux, tongue tie, or difficulty breastfeeding',
