  approachHeading="How we work"
  approach={[
    { title: 'Find the root cause', text: 'A comprehensive feeding evaluation across sensory, oral motor, airway, and behavioral factors — so we treat the reason, not just the symptom.' },
    { title: 'Safety before variety', text: 'We build trust with food through play and graded exposure — no force, no bribes, no pressure. Confidence first; new foods follow.' },
    { title: 'Whole-team support', text: 'Feeding therapists collaborate with our myofunctional, OT, and nutrition team, and coach you on routines, seating, and mealtime structure at home.' },
  ]}
  faq={[
    { q: 'What\'s the difference between picky eating and a feeding disorder?', a: 'Picky eating is common and often resolves; problem feeding is more restrictive, more distressing, and doesn\'t improve with time alone. If your child eats fewer than ~20 foods, drops foods permanently, or mealtimes are consistently stressful, an evaluation is worth it.' },
    { q: 'Do you work with babies and toddlers?', a: 'Yes — from infants transitioning to solids through school-age children. Earlier support is almost always easier and faster.' },
    { q: 'How is airway health related to feeding?', a: 'A child who can\'t breathe comfortably through their nose often can\'t chew and swallow comfortably either — and mouth breathing dulls appetite and taste. It\'s one of the most commonly missed drivers of picky eating, and a core part of our evaluation.' },
    { q: 'Will you coordinate with our pediatrician or GI?', a: 'Absolutely. We regularly collaborate with pediatricians, GIs, ENTs, lactation consultants, and dentists to keep everyone aligned.' },
